#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cargo-slot -- take one of two host-wide cargo build slots, then run cargo.

# What this is

`docs/open-questions.md` question 229 (the lead's ruling, round 4): three concurrent
`cargo`/`cargo test` jobs on this host serialise on one shared target-directory lock plus
macOS's own per-binary security scan (question 226) -- that is what makes the host slow, not
memory (the lead measured 64 GB RAM, 78 percent free, while three cargo jobs of one team ran
concurrently). The fix ratified there is a MECHANISM, not a convention every brief has to
remember on its own: `scripts/dev/cargo-slot` takes one of exactly two host-wide `flock(2)`
slots before running `cargo`, blocks (announcing the wait, never silently) if both are held, and
releases the slot the instant the `cargo` process it ran exits -- by any means, including
SIGKILL. Every brief uses it from round 5 on, in front of every `cargo` invocation:

    scripts/dev/cargo-slot test -p av-orbital
    scripts/dev/cargo-slot build --workspace
    scripts/dev/cargo-slot clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings

Arguments after `cargo-slot` are passed to `cargo` unchanged; this script's own exit status IS
cargo's exit status (see "How this releases the slot" below -- it `exec`s into cargo, it does
not fork a child and relay).

# Why TWO slots, not one

One shared lock (the docker-test lock's own pattern, `crates/av-lockstep/src/
docker_test_lock.rs` / `altavista/docker_test_lock.py`) would serialise ALL cargo jobs on the
host to one at a time. Question 229's own measurement says the host tolerates ONE FEWER than
the three jobs that were slow, not one: two concurrent cargo jobs is the ratified budget. So
there are two slot files, and a third caller waits for whichever of the two frees first.

# The lock paths

    $HOME/.altavista/locks/cargo-slot-0.lock
    $HOME/.altavista/locks/cargo-slot-1.lock

Created if absent, `flock(2) LOCK_EX`-locked exclusively -- the identical path family and
mechanism as `$HOME/.altavista/locks/docker-tests.lock` (`docker_test_lock.rs`'s own module
doc has the full case for `$HOME` specifically: Colima mounts only `$HOME` into its VM, and
macOS itself periodically reclaims `/tmp`/`/private/var/tmp`, so a lock outside `$HOME` is
unusable or transient for exactly the processes that need to agree on it forever). Read that
module in full before touching this one; this script is the same pattern with a second slot.

Why `flock` and not a PID-file scheme: a `flock` is attached to the *open file description*,
which the kernel tears down (releasing the lock) the instant a killed process's descriptors are
closed, with no userspace cleanup code involved at all. A PID file survives a SIGKILL as a
false "still held" record; `flock` does not have that failure mode. See `docker_test_lock.rs`'s
own "Why flock, not a lock-file-with-a-PID scheme" section for the full argument -- it applies
here unchanged.

# How this releases the slot (including on kill)

This script does NOT fork a child and wait on it. After it acquires a slot's `flock`, it calls
`os.execvp` -- replacing ITS OWN process image with `cargo`'s, in the same PID, keeping the
locked file descriptor open (explicitly marked inheritable across `exec` first; Python opens
file descriptors close-on-exec by default since PEP 446, which would otherwise drop the lock
the instant `cargo` started). There is deliberately no separate wrapper process left running
beside `cargo` that could be reaped or lost independently of it: whatever becomes of the process
running `cargo-slot test -p av-orbital` -- it runs to completion, or it is SIGKILLed -- IS what
happens to the process holding the lock, because after the `exec` they are the same process.
The kernel releases the `flock` the instant that process's file descriptors close, on any exit
path, with zero userspace code needing to run. Cargo's own exit status is therefore this
script's exit status, by construction, not by relaying one process's wait() result into another.

# The wait is announced, never silent (question 229's own citation of the docker-lock pattern)

Two non-blocking attempts happen first, in order: slot 0, then slot 1. If either succeeds,
that is the WHOLE acquisition -- silent, exactly as an uncontended `cargo` invocation looks
today. Only when BOTH are already held does this script say anything: ONE line to real stderr,
unbuffered (a raw `os.write(2, ...)`, never a `print`/`sys.stderr.write` that could sit in a
buffer -- the same reason `docker_test_lock.rs`'s `write_real_stderr` bypasses libtest's output
capture), naming both lock paths and citing `docs/open-questions.md` question 229, before this
script blocks. Once a slot is actually acquired, one more line reports the REAL, MEASURED
elapsed wait (`time.monotonic()`, never assumed, never the alarm's own tick count) -- so a human
watching a `cargo` run through this wrapper never mistakes a queued build for a hang.

# The blocking mechanism, and why it is NOT the plain SIGALRM recipe

The obvious approach -- install a `SIGALRM` handler, `signal.alarm(1)`, call the blocking
`fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_EX)`, and let the alarm interrupt it with `InterruptedError`/`EINTR` so
the wait can alternate between the two slots -- was tried and MEASURED not to work on this host
(macOS, the system `/usr/bin/python3`, 3.9.6) with a plain, non-raising signal handler: the
alarm fires (confirmed: the handler DOES run), but CPython's own PEP 475 EINTR auto-retry logic
silently resumes the interrupted `flock()` syscall inside the C implementation of
`fcntl.flock`, without ever returning control to Python. Measured directly: a waiter process
with a no-op `SIGALRM` handler, alarming every 1s against a lock held for 4s by another process,
never raised `InterruptedError` even once and simply returned "ACQUIRED" the moment the holder
released -- indistinguishable, from the waiter's own code, from a plain blocking call with no
alarm at all. A busy-poll was therefore never needed and was never measured as a fallback: the
actual fix is one line different from the naive recipe, not a different mechanism --

    def _on_alarm(signum, frame):
        raise _AlarmInterrupt()

A signal handler that RAISES bypasses PEP 475's retry (the retry loop only swallows EINTR when
the Python-level handler returns normally; an exception raised from the handler propagates out
of the interrupted syscall instead). Measured with this fix: the same 4s-held-lock scenario
produced three real interrupts at ~1.0s, ~2.0s, ~3.0s (each logged), then acquisition at ~3.98s
-- the process is parked in the kernel between alarms, not spinning, and alternates cleanly
between the two slot fds so whichever slot frees first is the one taken. No CPU-burning
`sleep`-poll loop exists anywhere in this script.

One race this costs, and how it is closed (found in the manager's round-4 review): a Python
signal handler runs at the next BYTECODE boundary, not inside the syscall, so the alarm can land
after `flock` has already returned successfully and before `signal.alarm(0)` cancels it. The
naive `except` then abandons a slot this process already holds and goes on to block on the other
one -- possibly for a whole other build's duration, while holding a slot it could have used.
`_blocking_wait_for_either_slot` therefore PROBES rather than assumes: on every interrupt it
re-attempts the same fd non-blocking, which succeeds both when the lock was in fact just acquired
(re-locking an fd this process already holds is a no-op) and when it has since become free, and
only switches slots when that probe genuinely reports the slot still held by someone else.

# Test-only overrides (never read by, or meant for, a production `cargo-slot` invocation)

`AV_CARGO_SLOT_DIR` -- overrides the lock directory (default `$HOME/.altavista/locks`) so a
test can point this script at a private scratch directory it owns instead of the real,
host-wide slots (`docs/open-questions.md` question 212(b): a test must never depend on, or
contend for, the real shared lock).

`AV_CARGO_SLOT_EXE` -- overrides the executable this script `exec`s into (default `cargo`), so
a test can hold a slot for a controlled, measurable duration with a harmless stand-in command
instead of a real build. Production use never sets this.

# Hard failures, never a silent no-lock fallback

If `$HOME` is unset (and `AV_CARGO_SLOT_DIR` was not given), or the lock directory/files cannot
be created or opened, this script fails loudly and exits non-zero before ever running `cargo` --
the same "refuse, never silently proceed unlocked" rule `docker_test_lock.rs`'s own
`lock_file_path`/`lock_docker_tests` state and this task's own binding rule restates.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import fcntl
import os
import signal
import sys
import time

SLOT_COUNT = 2
DEFAULT_LOCK_SUBDIR = os.path.join(".altavista", "locks")
SLOT_FILENAME_TEMPLATE = "cargo-slot-{}.lock"
DIR_OVERRIDE_ENV = "AV_CARGO_SLOT_DIR"
EXE_OVERRIDE_ENV = "AV_CARGO_SLOT_EXE"
ALARM_INTERVAL_S = 1


def _fail(message: str) -> "None":
    """Hard error, never a silent no-lock fallback -- see this script's own module doc comment's
    "Hard failures" section."""
    _write_real_stderr(f"cargo-slot: {message}\n")
    sys.exit(1)


def _write_real_stderr(text: str) -> None:
    """A raw, unbuffered write to real stderr (fd 2) -- never `print`/`sys.stderr.write`, which
    can sit behind Python's own buffering or a test harness's output capture. Mirrors
    `crates/av-lockstep/src/docker.rs`'s `write_real_stderr` for the identical reason."""
    os.write(2, text.encode("utf-8"))


def _lock_dir() -> str:
    """`$HOME/.altavista/locks`, or `AV_CARGO_SLOT_DIR` (test-only, see module doc comment) if
    set. Reading the environment is fine (question 199 forbids only *mutating* it); never falls
    back to any other directory if `$HOME` is unset -- a lock this script cannot actually reach
    is worse than a hard failure at the call site."""
    override = os.environ.get(DIR_OVERRIDE_ENV)
    if override:
        return override
    home = os.environ.get("HOME")
    if not home:
        _fail(
            "$HOME is not set -- cannot locate the cargo-slot lock directory "
            f"({DEFAULT_LOCK_SUBDIR}); this is a hard error, not a silent no-lock fallback "
            "(docs/open-questions.md question 229). Set $HOME, or set "
            f"{DIR_OVERRIDE_ENV} (test-only) to a private directory."
        )
    return os.path.join(home, DEFAULT_LOCK_SUBDIR)


def _slot_paths(lock_dir: str) -> "list[str]":
    return [os.path.join(lock_dir, SLOT_FILENAME_TEMPLATE.format(i)) for i in range(SLOT_COUNT)]


def _open_slot_fds(lock_dir: str, paths: "list[str]") -> "list[int]":
    try:
        os.makedirs(lock_dir, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError as e:
        _fail(f"could not create the lock directory {lock_dir!r}: {e}")
    fds = []
    for path in paths:
        try:
            fds.append(os.open(path, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o644))
        except OSError as e:
            _fail(f"could not open/create the cargo-slot lock file {path!r}: {e}")
    return fds


class _AlarmInterrupt(Exception):
    """Raised from the `SIGALRM` handler installed in `_blocking_wait_for_either_slot` -- see
    this script's own module doc comment's "The blocking mechanism" section for why the handler
    must RAISE rather than merely return (a no-op handler's alarm is silently swallowed by
    CPython's PEP 475 EINTR auto-retry inside `fcntl.flock` itself, measured directly)."""


def _blocking_wait_for_either_slot(fds: "list[int]") -> int:
    """Blocks until EITHER slot's `flock(LOCK_EX)` can be taken, alternating a 1-second
    `SIGALRM`-bounded blocking attempt between the two slot file descriptors so whichever frees
    first is the one acquired. Returns the index of the acquired slot. The process is parked in
    the kernel between alarms (a real blocking syscall), never spinning in a `sleep` loop -- see
    the module doc comment for the measurement that ruled out the naive no-op-handler recipe."""

    def _on_alarm(signum, frame):
        raise _AlarmInterrupt()

    old_handler = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, _on_alarm)
    try:
        idx = 0
        while True:
            signal.alarm(ALARM_INTERVAL_S)
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fds[idx], fcntl.LOCK_EX)
                signal.alarm(0)
                return idx
            except _AlarmInterrupt:
                # The alarm can land in the window BETWEEN `flock` returning successfully and
                # `signal.alarm(0)` running -- a Python-level signal handler only runs at the
                # next bytecode boundary, which is after the syscall has already succeeded. In
                # that case this process ALREADY HOLDS `fds[idx]`, and blindly moving on to the
                # other slot would leave it blocking (possibly for a whole other build) on slot
                # `1 - idx` while holding slot `idx` it could have used. So probe, never assume:
                # a non-blocking re-attempt on the SAME fd succeeds both when the lock was in
                # fact just acquired (re-locking an fd this process already holds is a no-op)
                # and when it has since become free -- either way this process holds it and the
                # wait is over. No alarm is pending here: it is the one that just fired.
                try:
                    fcntl.flock(fds[idx], fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                    return idx
                except BlockingIOError:
                    pass
                idx = (idx + 1) % SLOT_COUNT
                continue
    finally:
        signal.alarm(0)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, old_handler)


def _acquire_a_slot(fds: "list[int]", paths: "list[str]") -> int:
    """Tries slot 0 then slot 1 non-blocking. If both are already held, announces the wait
    (naming both paths, citing question 229) before blocking, then announces the real, measured
    elapsed wait once a slot is actually acquired. Never blocks silently."""
    for idx, fd in enumerate(fds):
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            return idx
        except BlockingIOError:
            continue
        except OSError as e:
            _fail(f"flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on {paths[idx]!r} failed for a reason other than blocking: {e}")

    _write_real_stderr(
        "WAITING for a cargo-slot ({} , {}): both host-wide slots are currently held by other "
        "processes on this host -- blocking until one releases (docs/open-questions.md question "
        "229: two cargo jobs may run concurrently on this host, not three; a third waits for "
        "whichever of the two slots frees first)\n".format(paths[0], paths[1])
    )
    waited_since = time.monotonic()
    idx = _blocking_wait_for_either_slot(fds)
    waited_s = time.monotonic() - waited_since
    _write_real_stderr(
        f"ACQUIRED cargo-slot {idx} ({paths[idx]}) after waiting {waited_s:.3f}s\n"
    )
    return idx


def main() -> "None":
    args = sys.argv[1:]

    lock_dir = _lock_dir()
    paths = _slot_paths(lock_dir)
    fds = _open_slot_fds(lock_dir, paths)

    acquired_idx = _acquire_a_slot(fds, paths)

    # Close every slot fd this process did not acquire -- only the winning lock should be held,
    # and nothing else needs to travel into the exec'd process.
    for idx, fd in enumerate(fds):
        if idx != acquired_idx:
            os.close(fd)

    acquired_fd = fds[acquired_idx]
    # Python opens file descriptors close-on-exec by default (PEP 446). Explicitly mark this one
    # inheritable so the flock it holds survives into the exec'd cargo process -- see the module
    # doc comment's "How this releases the slot" section.
    os.set_inheritable(acquired_fd, True)

    exe = os.environ.get(EXE_OVERRIDE_ENV, "cargo")
    try:
        os.execvp(exe, [exe] + args)
    except OSError as e:
        _fail(f"could not exec {exe!r}: {e}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
